 Taxonomy 
Hypericum grandifolium was first formally described by Jacques Denys Choisy in 1821. He collected the species' type specimen on the island of Tenerife. This description was one of the first that dealt with Macaronesian flora spanning multiple island groups. In it, Choisy gave {{nowrap|'' H. grandifolium''}} the following brief identifying description:{{Text and translation|''Hypericum'' caule tereti, foliis magnis amplexicaulibus, calyce in pedunculum reflexo, corolla sublineari. Caulis fruticosus, erectus, rubricans, teres, ramosus; rami obliqui. |''Hypericum'' with a cylindrical stem, large embracing leaves, calyx bent back on the peduncle, and sublinear corolla. The stem is shrubby, erect, reddening, cylindrical, and branching; the branches are oblique.}}{{cladogram|{{Clade|style=font-size:100%;line-height:100%;width:300px;
